    kg:textSpan "database as a complementary path to scaling language models. Instead of increasing the size of the model and training on more data, we equip models with the ability to directly access a large database to perform predictionsa semi-parametric approach. At a high level, our Retrieval Transformer ( Retro ) model splits the input sequence into chunks and retrieves text similar to the previous chunk to improve the predictions in the current chunk. Existing retrieval for language modelling work only co" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2112_04426_rag__chunk_011> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2112.04426_rag::chunk_011" ;
    kg:docId "2112.04426_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2112_04426_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "2021 ); Weidinger et al. ( 2021 ) highlight several dangers of large language models. Those stem from their ability to memorise training data, their high training cost, the static nature of their training data ( Lazaridou et al. , 2021 ), their tendency of amplifying inherent biases in the training data, and their ability to generate toxic language ( Gehman et al. , 2020 ). In this section we inspect these dangers, focusing on how retrieval augmented language models may exacerbate or 9 mitigate " .

